agonal down-right. Blocked at F6(X). So no 4-in-a-row possible there.  \n\nSo maybe build a new line:  \nPlay B4 (connects to C3 and D4? Not in straight line).  \n\nBetter: Create a new open three: Place O at G4. Then D4(O), E5(O), G4(O)? Not aligned.  \n\nActually, the strongest setup is to make a 4-in-a-row somewhere.  \n\nConsider:  \n- Row: No whites in same row yet.  \n- Column: No whites in same column yet.  \n- Diagonal: Only C3,D4,E5 are aligned but blocked at both ends.  \n\nSo maybe play near H8 to start new threat. But better is to block Black’s potential.  \n\nBlack’s possible threat: D3(X), D6(X) with D4(O) in between — no threat.  \n\nLet’s check Black’s diagonal A1–D4: A1(X), B2(X), C3(O) — broken.  \n\nActually, Black has two in a row at D3(X), D6(X) vertically, but D4(O) blocks.  \n\nGiven the board is open, I should play to create a **new open three** or **four**.  \n\nPossible move: **F5** — creates diagonal F5–H3? Not strong.  \n\nBetter: **B4** — connects to C3(O) diagonally up-left? C3 is row3 colC, B4 is row4 colB — different diagonal.  \n\nLet’s think: Play G6 — connects to H8(O)? No.  \n\nActually, the best is to extend C3–E5 diagonal by playing **B2**? B2 is X.  \n\nSo maybe play **F4** to prepare two diagonals:  \nF4 connects to E5 and D4 in diagonal up-left, and also to C3? C3–D4–E5–F4 is not straight. Wait:  \nC3 (3,C), D4 (4,D), E5 (5,E), F4 (4,F) — not same diagonal.  \n\nScratch that.  \n\nLet’s systematically find a move that gives a 4-in-a-row possibility:  \nPlay G7 — connects to H8 in row? No.  \n\nActually, the only white stones are C3, D4, E5, H8.  \nThe only cluster is C3,D4,E5 diagonal. Both ends blocked, so need to create a new line with two of them plus new stones.  \n\nIdea: Play B5 — connects to C3? Not straight.  \n\nBetter: Play G4 — connects to E5 and C3? Not aligned.  \n\nCheck: C3 and E5 are diagonal (difference row+2, col+2). So midpoint is D4 (already O). So that’s a three. To make four, need either B2 or F6, both X